{
  "entity0": {
    "rationale": "Iteration 0 has no race telemetry or integration feedback yet, so this is a baseline built from the contract. Five line sensors spaced 15 mm apart (x = -0.03 to +0.03) span 60 mm, more than twice the 25 mm tape width. That keeps at least one sensor on the tape across the lateral error range that stays inside the off-track limit, and gives the Integrator a graded signal rather than a binary one. Sensors sit 50 mm ahead of the axle so the signal reads the line before the wheels reach it, which matters at bends. Sensor size is 12 mm: smaller than the 25 mm tape so readings change smoothly across the edges, and large enough to avoid a noisy, near-binary response. Wheel track 0.12 m and wheel radius 0.035 m give a stable turn rate and enough wheel speed to reach the roughly 1.5 m/s average needed to finish in 3 s. Body width 0.12 m keeps the off-track limit at its 0.06 m floor, so it does not loosen the limit. Mass 0.7 kg is a moderate choice that keeps inertia low for quick steering while the drive keeps enough traction. I expect a baseline that completes the oval and s-bend, with the slalom as the hardest case because of the fast direction changes.",
    "geometry": {
      "sensor_positions": [
        {"x": -0.03, "y": 0.05},
        {"x": -0.015, "y": 0.05},
        {"x": 0.0, "y": 0.05},
        {"x": 0.015, "y": 0.05},
        {"x": 0.03, "y": 0.05}
      ],
      "sensor_size": 0.012,
      "wheel_track": 0.12,
      "wheel_radius": 0.035,
      "body_width": 0.12,
      "body_length": 0.18,
      "mass": 0.7
    },
    "intent": "Baseline symmetric five-sensor array with a 60 mm lateral span and forward placement at 50 mm ahead of the axle, on a light, moderately wide body with mid-size wheels. Purpose: a clean, well-conditioned platform that gives the Integrator an unambiguous signal, from which we can measure how sensor span and lead distance affect tracking error and completion time.",
    "feedback_request": {
      "measurements": [
        "completion",
        "progress",
        "completion_time",
        "rms_error",
        "max_error",
        "line_loss_events",
        "steering_oscillation"
      ],
      "questions_for_integrator": [
        "Does the five-sensor span saturate or go silent at any bend? Are any sensors frequently reading 0 or 1 together?",
        "Is the signal well-behaved across the full weight range, or does it need a much smaller weight scale than the sensor spacing suggests?",
        "Is the 0.12 m wheel track and 0.7 kg mass giving enough responsiveness at the slalom, or is the robot too slow to turn?",
        "Would more forward lead (larger y) or tighter spacing help the Integrator's derivative action?"
      ]
    },
    "learning": "Hypotheses to test: (1) a 60 mm sensor span gives enough signal across the slalom without losing the line; (2) a 50 mm forward lead lowers rms_error at bends compared with a sensor line at the axle; (3) a 0.035 m wheel radius with 0.7 kg mass reaches the 1.5 m/s average needed to finish in under 3 s. If completion fails, check whether the failure is speed (completion_time near 3 s) or line loss (line_loss_events high), and change the geometry accordingly in the next iteration.",
    "_presentation_note": "Baseline: five sensors, 60 mm span, 50 mm lead"
  },
  "_presentation": {
    "entity0": {
      "label": "Baseline 5-sensor array",
      "notes": "Symmetric five-sensor array spanning 60 mm, 50 mm ahead of the axle, on a light 0.7 kg body with 35 mm wheels. There is no race evidence yet, so this rests on the contract: the span covers more than twice the tape width, and the speed and off-track limits are checked."
    }
  }
}
